Back up to a sheet whose grid no longer has room, without going out of bounds

The chunked backup fixed the 50,000-character cell limit and then broke on the
next limit Google enforces: a tab has a fixed number of rows and columns.
saveBackup_ deleted the device's old rows and appendRow'd the new chunks, and
every upload came back "Those rows are out of bounds." - the sync log's own
words. appendRow does not grow a sheet, so on a full or trimmed tab it throws
exactly like a getRange reaching past the last row; deleteRow/deleteRows shift
everything below and are just as exposed at the last row. The tab had run out of
rows, and the write had never asked for more.

saveBackup_ now treats the grid as finite:
  - ensureRows_/ensureCols_ grow the sheet (insertRowsAfter/insertColumnsAfter)
    before any write that would reach outside it. sheet_ calls ensureCols_
    before touching row 1, because a 26-column tab can be narrower than a header.
  - The device's old rows are found by reading the sheet, then clearContent'd -
    never deleted, so no row below shifts and no delete can land out of bounds.
    getLastRow() is read after the clear, so cleared rows are not counted.
  - The new block is written in one getRange(...).setValues() at getLastRow()+1,
    with the grid grown first. One write means a failed write cannot leave a
    half-backup, and no grid arithmetic has to survive an intervening delete.

js/sync.js drops a device's superseded backup jobs once one actually lands: any
older FAILED or PENDING "Cloud backup" for that device is stale (it would only
overwrite the newer snapshot now in the sheet). Backups stamped later, and every
non-backup job, are left alone (syncDropSupersededBackups).

test/sheet.test.js's fake Sheet now enforces a finite grid and throws the same
"Those rows are out of bounds." / "Those columns are out of bounds." that Google
does, so this class of bug cannot pass the suite again. makeSheet gained
maxRows/maxCols and buildApi() loads Code.gs onto a spreadsheet a test chooses.
New cases: first backup on a fresh sheet; a snapshot needing more chunks than
the tab has rows (the tab is grown, the payload round-trips); a smaller second
backup upserting with no stale rows; three devices with two writes each; and a
legacy single-cell row read alongside chunked ones. These fail on the previous
Code.gs (3 FAIL, "Those rows are out of bounds.") and pass here.

The published version and APK are deliberately left at 2027-01-01.10. The
android-keystore.jks on this machine does not match the committed APK's signing
certificate, so rebuilding would re-sign the app with a different key and stop
it installing over the build already on the owner's phone. js/sync.js is served
network-first by the service worker, so this fix still reaches phones and the
web install without a version bump.

The Apps Script deployment is still manual: paste texpark-pro/Code.gs into the
project, Deploy > Manage deployments > edit > New version, same /exec URL and
"Who has access: Anyone".

Co-authored-by: openhands <openhands@all-hands.dev>
